I mean, yes, easily? Similar fictional universes existed long before the wiki, and several continue to exist separately from it.

I would also argue that Control isn't as much of a ripoff as you seem to suggest. It has very different specifics and also a very different approach to tone and storytelling.

+block

–block


For one, most of the objects are a lot more boring in-and-of themselves, because the narrative interest comes from their original backstories and, most importantly, the fun and creative missions that you get sucked into to  them. That also ties into Control's extradimensional/location-fuckery focus that is a huge core aspect of the setting and lore and source of the anomalies.

Take the fridge you have to stare at: It's almost nothing like 173 outside of that feature. It has to be watched or it will change and do nebulous bad things (Not just shit and snap necks), and when Jesse trys to tackle it she ends up in a treacherous boss fight with Former. Former has his own deep backstory and a major role in The Foundation DLC, which is about a literal Foundation, not us.

Honestly I found the world of Control to be a little boring and un-Foundation-y, because they were sticking with a few core canon elements and a single tone. (Extradimensionality and weird outside forces encroaching, elements of US conspiracy culture, a particular kind of weirdo vibe of the major human characters, and of course Jesse herself.)








